/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  column20_mtbe_kinetic_mesh -- column19's problem with a RATE-LIMITED
  reaction:   methanol + isobutene <=> MTBE   on an acid ion-exchange resin.

  THE PROBLEM is column19's, unchanged: 15 equilibrium stages, stage 1 the
  PARTIAL condenser, stage 15 the reboiler, P = 7 bar, reactive stages 6 to
  10, both feeds on stage 10, bottoms rate 240 kmol/h and boil-up ratio 5,
  UNIFAC liquid, SRK vapour, the same isobutene overlay.  What changes is
  the reaction: where column19 closes an equilibrium quotient on each
  reactive stage, here the extent of a stage is COMPUTED from a rate law and
  the catalyst it holds,  xi_j = r(T_j, a_j) m_cat,j,  with no extent unknown
  and still NO heat-of-reaction term (the energy rows price on the elements
  datum, which already holds it).

  THE LAW is Rehfinger & Hoffmann, Chem. Eng. Sci. 45 (1990) 1605-1617,
  doi 10.1016/0009-2509(90)80013-5, Amberlyst 15 (their Eq. 12):

      r = k ( a_IB / a_MeOH  -  a_MTBE / (K_a a_MeOH^2) )

  in liquid-phase ACTIVITIES, r per acid equivalent.  k(T) is their Eq. 15
  (k(90 C) = 243.8 mmol/(s eq), E = 92.4 kJ/mol, Table 4), K_a(T) their Eq. 6
  with K_a(298.15 K) = 300.5 (Table 4, the value the three-parameter fit
  returned, so that k and K_a are the PAIR the paper fitted; Eq. 6 with 284,
  the value of its Fig. 9 caption, reproduces Izquierdo's Table IV and is the
  better thermodynamics, and the difference is 5.8 %).  The capacity of
  Amberlyst 15, 4.9 meq/g, is the paper's (p. 1606).
  THE PAPER'S ACTIVITIES ARE UNIQUAC; THIS CASE'S ARE UNIFAC.  The law and the
  activities were fitted together, so the pairing here is an approximation
  that is stated, not repaired.

  THE CATALYST MASS (catalystMass) IS ILLUSTRATIVE: it is not any real
  column's loading.  It is spread equally over the five reactive stages, and
  the conversion that follows (about 9 % of the methanol) is a property of
  that choice AND of the law: the reactive stages run methanol-LEAN
  (x_MeOH 0.05-0.18, below the 0.3 the law was measured down to), where 1/a_MeOH
  is large but a_MeOH itself is small.  MEASURED while building the case: the
  continuation in the catalyst mass converges up to about 270 kg and stops at
  about 300 kg (s = 1 not reached), so this case does not claim a higher
  conversion than the engine reached.

  WHAT THE LAW DOES NOT CARRY: the effectiveness factor (internal diffusion
  in the resin) -- the paper itself says the measured rates exceed the law's
  below x_MeOH = 0.3, "caused by internal mass transfer".  The law diverges as
  a_MeOH -> 0 (1/a_MeOH, and 1/a_MeOH^2 on the reverse leg); no floor is
  hidden in the engine and none is written here: a trial that drives a_MeOH
  to zero is an INFEASIBLE trial (announced) and the Newton shortens its
  step.  The run publishes a_MeOH on every reactive stage (the `a_methanol`
  profile column) so it can be read against the range the law was measured
  over; `validity` announces a stage below x_MeOH = 0.3.

  Nothing here was read off, or fitted to, any simulator's result.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only

units
(
    {
        name        mtbeColumn;
        type        distillationColumn;
        model       fullMESH;
        inputs      ( methanolFeed  c4Feed );
        outputs     ( distillate  bottoms );

        operation
        {
            nStages        15;
            bottomsRate    240 kmol/h;
            boilupRatio    5;
            P              7 bar;
            condenser      { type partial; }

            feeds
            (
                { stream methanolFeed; stage 10; }
                { stream c4Feed;       stage 10; }
            );
        }

        reaction
        {
            stoichiometry ( { component methanol;              nu -1; }
                            { component isoButene;             nu -1; }
                            { component methylTertButylEther;  nu  1; } );
            reactiveStages ( 6 7 8 9 10 );
            kinetics
            {
                model        expression;
                catalystMass 220 kg;        // ILLUSTRATIVE, total, equal on the 5 stages
                source       "Rehfinger & Hoffmann, Chem. Eng. Sci. 45 (1990) 1605-1617, Eq. 12 (rate), Eq. 15 (k), Eq. 6 (K_a), Table 4 (k90, E, Ka298); capacity 4.9 meq/g p. 1606; activities there UNIQUAC, here UNIFAC";
                basis        catalystMass;
                rateOf       extent;

                parameters
                {
                    k90       0.2438    1/s;        // 243.8 mmol/(s eq) = 0.2438 mol per mol acid sites per s (Table 4)
                    E         92400     J/mol;      // Table 4 / Eq. 15
                    T90       363.15    K;          // Eq. 15 reference
                    RgK       8.314462  J/mol;      // R x 1 K
                    Kelvin    1         K;
                    capacity  4.9       mol/kg;     // A15 acid capacity, 4.9 meq/g (p. 1606)
                    Ka298     300.5     -;          // Table 4 (fitted with k and E)
                    T0        298.15    K;          // Eq. 6 reference
                    A1       -1492.77   K;          // Eq. 6
                    A2       -77.4002   -;
                    A3        [0 0 0 -1 0] 0.507563;
                    A4        [0 0 0 -2 0] -9.12739e-4;
                    A5        [0 0 0 -3 0] 1.10649e-6;
                    A6        [0 0 0 -4 0] -6.27996e-10;
                }

                definitions
                {
                    aMeOH    "a[methanol]";
                    aIB      "a[isoButene]";
                    aMTBE    "a[methylTertButylEther]";
                    xMeOH    "x[methanol]";
                    lnKfac   "A1*(1/T - 1/T0) + A2*ln(T/T0) + A3*(T - T0) + A4*(T*T - T0*T0) + A5*(T*T*T - T0*T0*T0) + A6*(T*T*T*T - T0*T0*T0*T0)";
                    Ka       "Ka298 * exp(lnKfac)";
                    k        "k90 * exp(-E / RgK * Kelvin * (1/T - 1/T90))";
                }

                rate         "k * capacity * (aIB / aMeOH - aMTBE / (Ka * aMeOH * aMeOH))";

                validity
                {
                    xMeOH { min 0.3;  outside announce; }
                }

                caveats
                (
                    "The law has no effectiveness factor: below x_MeOH = 0.3 the paper's measured rates exceed the law's (internal mass transfer), so the rate there is a lower bound."
                    "The law diverges as a_MeOH -> 0; a trial that reaches it is an infeasible Newton trial, not a number."
                );
            }
        }
    }
);
